window.EN = {
  "meta.title": "Does red light therapy work?",
  "top.home": "Renato Shirakashi",
  "top.kicker": "Red light · 2026",
  "hero.eyebrow": "Interactive essay · November 2026",
  "hero.h1": "\n    <span class=\"mark\">Does red light</span>\n    <span class=\"mark l2\">therapy work?</span>\n  ",
  "hero.sub": "Red light has become a craze in masks, panels and beds, with promises that run from wrinkles to testosterone. We read the studies, from cells to clinical trials, and some of those promises have solid ground, while others do not survive a skeptical look. Was Kim Kardashian right?",
  "hero.byline": "by Renato Shirakashi",
  "s00.step": "00 · The fad",
  "s00.h": "Searches for red light therapy grew about 23-fold since 2018 and hit a record in April 2026, driven by LED masks",
  "s00.p1": "At Christmas 2023, Kim Kardashian gave red <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"led\">LED</span> masks from the brands CurrentBody and Omnilux to family and friends and wrote in her Instagram stories that she was obsessed with them. The masks had been on sale for years, but it was from that holiday season on that worldwide Google searches for “red light therapy” moved to a new level. The yearly average of the search index, which had stayed between 3 and 8 from 2018 to 2022, rose to 25 in 2024 and to 46 in 2025, and reached 65 in 2026, about 23 times the 2018 average. The index jumps every year between October and December, when the masks become presents, and searches for “red light mask” hit their highest value in December 2025.",
  "s00.p2": "The record for “red light therapy” came in April 2026. Between March and May, the fastest-rising searches were for red light mats, caps, belts and beds, a sign that the fad had moved from the face to the whole body. By October the index had fallen by about 60%, to a level still three times that of October 2023. For comparison, the ice bath (“cold plunge”), another wellness fad of the same period, reached 24 on the scale on which red light reached 100.",
  "s00.p3": "In Brazil, almost nobody searches for “terapia de luz vermelha”. Brazilians look for “<span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"lllt\">laserterapia</span>”, an old term from physiotherapy, dentistry and aesthetics that has grown steadily since 2018, and for “LED facial”, which peaked in July 2026. The English-language wave passed through the country between November 2025 and June 2026 and had almost vanished by October, in a low-volume series with signs of automated traffic.",
  "s00.p4": "Manufacturers followed the demand. The FDA, the American agency that regulates medical devices, cleared 2 to 8 light devices for wrinkles a year between 2008 and 2021, 16 in 2024 and 42 in 2025. CurrentBody, one of the brands in Kardashian’s presents, made £125.8 million from the masks in 2025, about 89% of the revenue of the group that owns it, which listed on the London Stock Exchange in October of that year. The chart below puts the searches and the clearances together:",
  "l00.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Google searches for red light and devices cleared by the FDA, 2018 to 2026</b><span class=\"wdo\">Follow the top line to the April 2026 peak and compare each year-end jump with the columns of clearances below.</span>",
  "l00.cap": "Google Trends, queries of 11/14/2026 (pytrends). The index runs from 0 to 100, and 100 is the month with the most searches within each query; the worldwide and Brazil panels cannot be compared with each other. FDA, openFDA 510(k) database, product code OHS. Marie Claire, on Kardashian’s presents.",
  "s00.p5": "The people who promote these masks on social media are rarely doctors. In a survey of 132 posts published in February 2026, 64.4% came from people with no health training, and only 8.3% linked to a peer-reviewed study (Merkle and colleagues, <em>Cureus</em>). The promises range from wrinkles and acne to hair loss, belly fat, testosterone, sleep and the “energy of the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"mitocondria\">mitochondria</span>”. Some have solid studies behind them and others have none, and to tell them apart one needs to know what this light is and what happens to it once it enters the body.",
  "s01.step": "01 · The light",
  "s01.h": "Red and near infrared light pass through skin better than other colors, because blood and water absorb little of them",
  "s01.p1": "The red light from these devices is ordinary light, of the same nature as the light from a lamp, concentrated in a narrow band of colors. The color of light depends on its <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"comprimento\">wavelength</span>, measured in nanometers (nm), which are millionths of a millimeter. The eye sees from about 400 nm, violet, to about 700 nm, the darkest red. Above 700 nm begins the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"nir\">near infrared</span>, which the eye cannot see. Red light devices almost always emit at two or three points on this ruler, and the numbers printed on the boxes are 630, 660, 810, 830 and 850 nm.",
  "s01.p2": "To have any effect on the body, light has to be absorbed by some molecule, and most colors are absorbed before they get far. Blood holds back blue and green, and oxygen-carrying <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"hemoglobina\">hemoglobin</span> absorbs about 117 times more green light, at 550 nm, than red light, at 650 nm (Prahl’s tables, Oregon Medical Laser Center). <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"melanina\">Melanin</span>, the skin’s pigment, also absorbs the short colors more. At the other end of the ruler, water starts to absorb strongly beyond 950 nm, and at 980 nm it absorbs about 20 times more than at 800 nm (Hale and Querry, 1973). Between these two barriers lies a band, from about 650 to 950 nm, in which little light is absorbed, so it scatters through the tissue and goes deeper. Physicists call this band the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"janela\">optical window</span>, and every red light device works inside it:",
  "l01.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">The colors that blood, melanin and water let through</b><span class=\"wdo\">Follow the three curves from left to right and look for the valley between 650 and 950 nm, where the devices’ marks sit.</span>",
  "l01.cap": "Hemoglobin: Prahl, Oregon Medical Laser Center (omlc.org). Water: Hale and Querry (<em>Applied Optics</em>, 1973). Melanin: approximate shape, calculated for this essay. Peaks in HeLa cells: Karu and Kolyakov (<em>Photomed Laser Surg</em>, 2005). Optical window as in Jacques (<em>Phys Med Biol</em>, 2013).",
  "s01.p3": "The ruler also helps explain the numbers on the boxes. Karu and Kolyakov (2005) lit cultured human cells with one color at a time and measured how much more DNA they made and how well they stuck to the dish. The responses peaked in four bands, and the 630 and 660 nm LEDs sit near the first two, while the 850 nm LED sits just above the last. Manufacturers probably also choose these wavelengths because they match the cheapest LEDs on the market, an assumption of this essay that no company has confirmed. The peaks show where the cells responded, but they say nothing about how much light a person needs to receive or how deep it needs to reach.",
  "s02.step": "02 · How far it goes",
  "s02.h": "Red light loses almost everything in the first centimeter of skin, and near infrared gets a little deeper",
  "s02.p1": "Anyone who has held a phone flashlight behind their fingers has seen the hand glow red. The light that comes out the other side is red because the blood absorbed the blue and green on the way, and it comes out faint and scattered in every direction. The glow gives the impression that light passes through the body easily, but the measurements show that almost all of it is lost on the way.",
  "s02.p2": "In 2012, Jagdeo and colleagues pointed a commercial LED at the cheek of living volunteers and measured how much light got through about 1 cm of tissue. Before you see the result, choose how much of the red light you think reached the other side:",
  "l02.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How much light gets through a cheek, a hand and a skull</b><span class=\"wdo\">Choose how much red light gets through 1 cm of cheek. Then compare red with infrared in the three cross sections and in the skull bars.</span>",
  "l02.cap": "Jagdeo et al. (<em>PLoS One</em>, 2012), cheek and hand of living volunteers and a cadaver head; percentages calculated for this essay from the paper’s tables. Lapchak et al. (2015), dry skulls. Salehpour et al. (2019), review. Depths calculated for this essay with average skin parameters (Jacques, 2013). Cadavers and dry skulls have no circulating blood, which absorbs more light.",
  "s02.p3": "Of the red light at 633 nm, 2.1% got through, about 21 parts in every 1,000. Of the infrared at 830 nm, 12% got through, almost six times more. In the palm of the hand, 25 mm thick, about 1 part in 1,000 of the infrared got through and 1 in 20,000 of the red. The other measurements give the same order of magnitude. In a cadaver head, 808 nm infrared fell to about 11% with each centimeter and was still detected 4 cm below the surface (Tedford and colleagues, 2015). In a skin simulation, 750 nm light fell to 1% of its peak at a depth of about 5.4 mm (Ash and colleagues, 2017). That is why visible red light acts in the first few millimeters, where the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"epiderme\">epidermis</span> and the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"derme\">dermis</span> are, while near infrared reaches a few centimeters with large losses.",
  "s02.p4": "This loss matters for the devices that promise to reach the brain, such as the infrared helmets sold for memory and mood. A mouse skull has less than half a millimeter of bone and let through 40.1% of 800 nm light, while a dry human skull, 6 to 7 mm thick, let through 4.2% (Lapchak and colleagues, 2015). With scalp and soft tissue, a cadaver’s temple let through 0.9% of the infrared and almost none of the red. <span class=\"mark\">With the same device, a mouse’s brain receives 10 to 300 times more light than a person’s, and a 2019 review concluded that this gap casts doubt on the use of animals as a model (Salehpour and colleagues).</span>",
  "s03.step": "03 · In the cell",
  "s03.h": "In cells, red light makes mitochondria produce more energy, and in people this effect has not yet been measured directly",
  "s03.p1": "The first hint that this light changes something in the body came by chance. In 1967, in Budapest, the physician Endre Mester wanted to know whether the laser, invented seven years earlier, caused cancer. He shaved the backs of mice and lit one group with a weak ruby laser, at 694 nm. The paper, published in German in 1968, reported that no mouse developed cancer and that the hair of the lit group grew faster than that of the others. The technique became known as low-level laser therapy, and in 2014 a meeting of two societies in the field proposed changing the name to <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"pbm\">photobiomodulation</span>, because most devices already used LEDs and lamps instead of lasers. In PubMed, the database of medical papers, “photobiomodulation” overtook the old name in 2019. “Red light therapy”, the name that circulates on social media, appeared in 1 paper in 2010 and in 67 in 2025.",
  "s03.p2": "The most accepted explanation for the effect begins in the mitochondrion, the part of the cell that uses oxygen to produce <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"atp\">ATP</span>, the molecule in which the cell stores energy. The last enzyme in the chain that does this work, <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"cco\">cytochrome c oxidase</span>, has copper and iron atoms that absorb red and near infrared light, and when purified it absorbs most at 835 nm (Mason and colleagues, 2014). The most cited hypothesis says that, in stressed cells, <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"no\">nitric oxide</span> binds to this enzyme and slows the cell’s respiration, and that light releases the nitric oxide and lets the enzyme go back to work (de Freitas and Hamblin, 2016). After that would come a short pulse of <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"ros\">reactive oxygen species</span>, molecules that in small amounts switch on the cell’s defense genes (Chen and colleagues, 2011).",
  "s03.p3": "Part of this chain has been measured. In mitochondria isolated from rat liver, the light of a helium-neon laser raised ATP synthesis as early as 1984 (Passarella and colleagues), and in cell cultures the rise in ATP has been repeated in several labs. In 11 volunteers, a 1,064 nm laser pointed at the forehead raised the oxidized form of cytochrome c oxidase, measured by spectroscopy, compared with placebo (Wang and colleagues, 2017), and warming the skin by the same amount had no such effect (Wang and colleagues, 2018).",
  "s03.p4": "Other measurements contradict this chain. In 2019, Lima and colleagues used cells that had no assembled cytochrome c oxidase at all, and 660 nm light increased their multiplication as much as that of normal cells. In 2020, Quirk and Whelan wrote that no reliable demonstration of an effect of light on this enzyme had been published. The statement carries weight because Harry Whelan is the researcher who, in the 1990s, brought into medicine the LEDs that NASA was testing for growing plants. Among the studies gathered for this essay, none measured ATP in a person’s tissue after a session with a home device. The chart below shows what was measured at each level:",
  "l03.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">What light does in the mitochondrion and at what level each step was measured</b><span class=\"wdo\">Read the chain at the top from left to right and then go down the table, where each row says where that step was measured.</span>",
  "l03.cap": "Mason, Nicholls and Cooper (<em>BBA</em>, 2014); Karu and Kolyakov (2005); Karu et al. (<em>Lasers Surg Med</em>, 2005); Passarella et al. (<em>FEBS Lett</em>, 1984); Chen et al. (<em>PLoS One</em>, 2011); Sharma et al. (<em>Lasers Surg Med</em>, 2011); Huang et al. (<em>Dose Response</em>, 2011); Wang et al. (<em>J Cereb Blood Flow Metab</em>, 2017; <em>Neurophotonics</em>, 2018); Lima et al. (2019); Quirk and Whelan (<em>Photomed Laser Surg</em>, 2020); de Freitas and Hamblin (<em>IEEE J Sel Top Quantum Electron</em>, 2016).",
  "s03.p5": "The whole chain is plausible, and each link has data at some level. The firmest data come from cells in a dish, which receive the light without skin, blood and melanin in the way, while in people most of the steps still rest on inference. The field is also small and concentrated in a few groups, and coauthors of several of the most cited reviews declare ties to laser manufacturers.",
  "s04.step": "04 · The dose",
  "s04.h": "At the right dose, light helped wounds close, and at a dose 25 times higher it left the wound worse than no light at all",
  "s04.p1": "Two numbers describe how much light a tissue received. <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"irradiancia\">Irradiance</span>, in milliwatts per square centimeter (mW/cm²), says how much light reaches each square centimeter of skin each second, and it is the number the manufacturer should print on the box. The <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"dose\">dose</span>, in joules per square centimeter (J/cm²), adds up that light over time and equals the irradiance multiplied by the seconds. A 30 mW/cm² device switched on for 10 minutes delivers 18 J/cm², because 0.03 W/cm² times 600 seconds gives 18.",
  "s04.p2": "In 2007, Demidova-Rice and colleagues made wounds on the backs of mice and lit them with the same device, at 635 nm and 100 mW/cm², for different lengths of time, to deliver 1, 2, 10 or 50 J/cm². At this irradiance, 2 J/cm² takes 20 seconds, and 50 J/cm² takes 8 minutes and 20 seconds. Choose the dose you think closed the wound fastest:",
  "l04.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How much light time helped a wound close and how much got in the way</b><span class=\"wdo\">Choose one of the four light times and see where it fell on the curve. Then compare where the best result sits in the other studies, below.</span>",
  "l04.cap": "Demidova-Rice et al. (<em>Photomed Laser Surg</em>, 2007); Chen et al. (2011); Zhang et al. (2003); Hawkins and Abrahamse (2005); Sharma et al. (2011); Yamaura et al. (2009); Lopes-Martins et al. (2005); Huang et al. (<em>Dose Response</em>, 2009 and 2011), the source of these studies’ numbers. The dashed curves show the shape and are not fits to the data. Panel doses calculated from measurements by market reviewers.",
  "s04.p3": "<span class=\"mark\">The dose that helped most was 2 J/cm², twenty seconds of light, and at 50 J/cm², a little over eight minutes of the same device, the wounds grew more than those of the mice that received no light at all.</span>",
  "s04.p4": "This pattern has an old name. In 1877 and 1888, Hugo Schulz saw that small doses of poisons stimulated yeast and large doses inhibited it, and the idea that a weak stimulus activates and a strong one inhibits later got the name <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"hormese\">hormesis</span>. With light, researchers call the pattern a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"bifasica\">biphasic response</span>. It appears in cells, in wounds and in mouse brains, always with the peak in a different place. In mice with head injuries, 36 J/cm² improved the outcome for four weeks, and ten times more light removed the benefit and made the first six days worse than no treatment. Repetition also counts, because 14 daily sessions erased the advantage that one to three sessions had given (Huang and colleagues, 2011). Tissues with many mitochondria, such as muscle, nerve and brain, tend to respond to lower doses than skin and tendon, and in them the studies that found no effect seem to have erred more by too much light than by too little (Zein, Selting and Hamblin, 2018).",
  "s04.p5": "The dose does not tell the whole story either, because the speed at which the light arrives changes the result. In pressure ulcers in mice, Lanzafame and colleagues (2007) always gave 5 J/cm² a day and changed only the irradiance, and between 0.7 and 40 mW/cm² only 8 mW/cm² had a significant effect (Huang and colleagues, 2009). Since the same dose given slowly and given quickly can give different results, any calculation of minutes serves to estimate the order of magnitude, without hitting the exact point.",
  "s04.p6": "Almost all of this was measured in cells and animals. By 2009, no clinical study had shown the curve in people (Huang and colleagues, 2011). For clinical use, WALT, the World Association for Laser Therapy, published dose tables by diagnosis in 2010, from 4 to 16 joules per treated site, split into a few points, with 20 to 300 seconds per point and a margin of 50% either way. Outside that margin, the table itself says, the application no longer counts as low-level laser therapy, and the doses were calibrated for white skin. In the LED skin studies, not even the researchers agree on the dose. A 2025 review gathered 27 of these studies and found doses from 0.1 to 126 J/cm², with a median of 40.5, and none of them checked with a meter the dose it claimed to have delivered (Grimes).",
  "s05.step": "05 · Where it works",
  "s05.h": "Red light has a clinical guideline for preventing mouth sores in people treated for cancer, and a moderate effect against baldness and neck pain",
  "s05.p1": "The name “red light” covers a 660 nm laser held against the mouth, an infrared helmet, a laser comb for the scalp and an LED mask, and the doses of these devices differ by orders of magnitude. That is why the question of what works needs a target. The cards below hold six common uses. Drag each one to the rung you think it occupies, from the firmest, where a medical guideline recommends the use, to the weakest, where there is no study in people:",
  "l05.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">The evidence ladder for red light, use by use</b><span class=\"wdo\">Drag the six cards to the rung you imagine and tap <code>check</code>. The cards move to the right rung, and the other uses appear after them.</span>",
  "l05.cap": "Rungs classified for this essay from Zadik et al. (<em>Support Care Cancer</em>, 2019, MASCC/ISOO); Oberoi et al. (<em>PLoS One</em>, 2014); Lueangarun et al. (2021); Chow et al. (<em>Lancet</em>, 2009); Stausholm et al. (<em>BMJ Open</em>, 2019); Naterstad et al. (<em>BMJ Open</em>, 2022); Jiang et al. (<em>Ophthalmology</em>, 2022); FDA DEN230083; Barbaric et al. (Cochrane, 2016); Wunsch and Matuschka (2014); Li et al. (2018); Dutra et al. (2022); Jackson et al. (2009); Chun et al. (2025); Seok et al. (2026); Zhao et al. (2012); Höfling et al. (2013); Rasul et al. (2026). Standardized difference: 0.2 is small and 0.8 is large.",
  "s05.p2": "The top rung has a single use, and it belongs to a hospital laser. Oral <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"mucosite\">mucositis</span> is an inflammation with sores in the mouth and throat caused by chemotherapy and radiotherapy, and in severe cases the sores hurt so much that the patient cannot eat. In 2019, MASCC/ISOO, the international society that brings together the specialists on the subject, began recommending photobiomodulation to prevent mucositis in three situations: <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"tcth\">blood stem cell transplant</span>, head and neck radiotherapy without chemotherapy, and head and neck radiotherapy with chemotherapy. The recommendation comes with protocols for wavelength, power and time, and the guideline says the clinician should follow every parameter of the chosen protocol.",
  "s05.p3": "One of the central trials was done in Brazil. At the National Cancer Institute (INCA), in Rio de Janeiro, Antunes and colleagues randomly assigned 94 head and neck patients on chemoradiotherapy to receive a 660 nm laser or an identical device switched off, without patients or assessors knowing who got what. Each point in the mouth received 1 joule, about 10 seconds of a 100 mW laser, which gives about 4 J/cm² on the small area of the beam:",
  "l06.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How many of 100 head and neck patients had severe mucositis with and without the laser</b><span class=\"wdo\">Compare the two grids. Each figure is a patient, and the colored ones had severe mouth sores.</span>",
  "l06.cap": "Antunes et al. (<em>Oral Oncology</em>, 2013), phase III, placebo-controlled, <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"duplocego\">double-blind</span> trial, 94 patients at INCA; follow-up by Antunes et al. (2017); Oberoi et al. (<em>PLoS One</em>, 2014); Hu et al. (<em>Support Care Cancer</em>, 2026), funding not stated in the abstract. The 100 figures round the trial’s percentages.",
  "s05.p4": "Without the laser, 40.5% of patients had severe mucositis, and with it 6.4% did, about one sixth of the risk (Antunes and colleagues, 2013). A <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"meta\">meta-analysis</span> of 18 trials with 1,144 patients put the risk of severe mucositis at 0.37 times that of the control group, and severe mucositis lasted about five days less (Oberoi and colleagues, 2014). There was a fear that the light might stimulate the tumor in the mouth, and the follow-up of the same Brazilian trial found the opposite, with more complete responses in the laser group, in a small number of patients. In 2025, Brazil’s Ministry of Health added photobiomodulation for oral mucositis to the procedure table of the SUS, the public health system. Even this rung has limits. For treating mucositis that has already appeared, and for mucositis from chemotherapy alone, MASCC/ISOO says there is no evidence for any recommendation, and a 2024 review also rated most meta-analyses on the subject as of critically low quality (Rodrigues and colleagues).",
  "s05.p5": "The rung below it already has smaller effects and more caveats. In male and female pattern <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"alopecia\">baldness</span>, laser combs and helmets increased hair density compared with <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"sham\">sham light</span> devices in seven trials (Lueangarun and colleagues, 2021), with an effect comparable in size to that of <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"minoxidil\">minoxidil</span>. The first of these devices was cleared by the FDA in 2007. Almost all these trials were paid for by the manufacturers, lasted up to six months and do not say what happens when the person stops. A consensus of 21 experts published in 2025 in the <em>Journal of the American Academy of Dermatology</em> put baldness on the list of effective uses, along with <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"neuropatia\">peripheral neuropathy</span>, ulcers of various causes and the dermatitis caused by radiotherapy, and wrinkles and acne were left off that list (Maghfour and colleagues).",
  "s05.p6": "Pain is the case where meta-analysis and guideline disagree. For neck pain, 16 trials gave an average of 19.9 mm less on a 0 to 100 scale (Chow and colleagues, 2009, <em>Lancet</em>), about 2 points on a 0 to 10 scale. For knee <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"artrose\">osteoarthritis</span>, 22 trials gave 14.2 mm less (Stausholm and colleagues, 2019), and even so NICE, the British agency that writes the guidelines for the public health system, lists laser among the therapies that should not be offered for osteoarthritis. The difference a patient notices lies between 10 and 20 mm, so these effects sit at the edge of what can be felt. The authors of both meta-analyses belong to the core of WALT, the association that promotes the technique.",
  "s05.p7": "The eyes have two new uses. In dry <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"dmri\">macular degeneration</span>, the loss of central vision common after 60, the Valeda device received FDA <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"denovo\">De Novo</span> authorization in November 2024. In the only large trial, treated eyes read 3.8 more letters than eyes given sham light at 21 months, less than one line of the eye doctor’s letter chart, which has five. The sham group also improved, no non-white patient received the treatment, and the authors work for the manufacturer or consult for it. In childhood <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"miopia\">myopia</span>, a Chinese trial with 264 children aged 8 to 13 used 3 minutes of 650 nm light twice a day. In one year, the eye grew 0.13 mm in the light group and 0.38 mm in the group with ordinary glasses (Jiang and colleagues, 2022). The effect was repeated in other trials, also without a sham device in the control group, and during a three-month pause the eye grew faster in the light group than in a group treated with <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"atropina\">atropine</span> (Xie and colleagues, 2026). The safety of these devices is the subject of section 09.",
  "s06.step": "06 · Where it does not convince",
  "s06.h": "Wrinkles, acne, fat and the brain have small studies, often paid for by manufacturers, and testosterone and longevity have no studies in people at all",
  "s06.p1": "Masks mostly sell smoother skin. The most cited trial on wrinkles, by Wunsch and Matuschka (2014), treated 113 people with red light in 30 sessions and measured improvement in skin texture and in <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"colageno\">collagen</span> density by ultrasound. The comparison group had 23 people who received nothing, not even a sham light, so part of the improvement may come from the expectation and attention the treated group received. The other facial LED trials have 20 to 60 people. Experts interviewed by NPR in April 2026 described the improvement in wrinkles as real and small, smaller than that of an in-office laser, and possible only with months of use several times a week.",
  "s06.p2": "Acne has more studies and weaker numbers. A 2016 <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"cochrane\">Cochrane review</span> gathered 71 trials with 4,211 people, with a median of 31 participants per trial, and no light used alone showed a clinically relevant effect. Most of the positive results came from <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"pdt\">photodynamic therapy</span>, which combines light with a drug applied to the skin and therefore counts as a different treatment (Barbaric and colleagues). A 2023 meta-analysis of LEDs calculated a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"smd\">standardized difference</span> of 2.42 for acne, an effect too large for a cosmetic treatment and typical of small, unblinded studies.",
  "s06.p3": "The promises for the body sit even lower. Erchonia’s Zerona device received FDA clearance to reduce the circumference of the waist, hips and thighs. In a trial with authors linked to the company, the sum of the four measurements fell 8.9 cm in the treated group and 1.7 cm in the sham group, a difference of about 1.8 cm at each site (Jackson and colleagues, 2009). Two weeks later, the treated group had regained part of those measurements. In an independent test of the same device in 5 people, treating only one side of the body, dermatologists who did not know which side had been treated were right 51.1% of the time, the same as flipping a coin (Elm and colleagues, 2011). For exercise performance, a meta-analysis of 37 crossover trials with sham light found a small effect on the endurance of a single muscle and none on strength, running or swimming (Dutra and colleagues, 2022). Many of these trials came from a group at Uninove, in São Paulo, linked to the device maker Multi Radiance.",
  "s06.p4": "The brain, sleep and hormones have even less. The largest published helmet trial for memory had 26 participants (Chun and colleagues, 2025), and the Vielight trial, planned for 228 patients with Alzheimer’s, is listed as suspended, with no results. Almost all the marketing of “red light at night improves sleep” comes from a study of 20 Chinese female basketball players in which the comparison group received no light at all (Zhao and colleagues, 2012). A dim red light at night disrupts melatonin less than a white or blue light, but disrupting it less is a long way from making the body produce more melatonin. For testosterone, no trial in people has pointed red light at the testicles, and urologists point out that heat on the testicles tends to reduce sperm production. For longevity and “mitochondrial health”, there is no trial in people with any aging outcome. The chart below sets the main trials side by side, with their size, the type of comparison and who was behind them:",
  "l07.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How many people each use tested, against what, and who was behind it</b><span class=\"wdo\">Read each row from left to right and compare the circles at the top, the hospital uses, with those at the bottom, the advertised uses.</span>",
  "l07.cap": "Oberoi et al. (2014); Antunes et al. (2013); Chow et al. (2009); Jiang et al. (2022); Wunsch and Matuschka (2014); Boyer et al. (<em>Retina</em>, 2024) and Jaffe et al. (2026), LIGHTSITE III; Jackson et al. (2009); Höfling et al. (2013); Barbaric et al. (Cochrane, 2016); Chun et al. (2025); Zhao et al. (2012); Son et al. (<em>Syst Rev</em>, 2025). Ties as stated in the papers’ declarations and in the press cited in the sources.",
  "s06.p5": "Sham light is hard to do well, because red light is visible and some devices get warm, and volunteers can tell which group they are in. Trials that compare against “no treatment” or “no light” lack this protection, and it is precisely in the advertised uses that they appear most. The largest recent <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"umbrella\">synthesis</span> gathered 15 meta-analyses, 204 trials and more than 9,000 people, and found <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"grade\">moderate certainty</span> for only 5 outcomes. They were pain in burning mouth syndrome, disability in knee osteoarthritis, fatigue in fibromyalgia, hair density and cognitive function, which mixed healthy people and people with dementia (Son and colleagues, 2025). No line of that synthesis deals with aging or with mitochondria as an outcome.",
  "s07.step": "07 · The devices",
  "s07.h": "The minutes of a session depend on the light that reaches the skin, and that light is usually lower than the box says and falls with distance",
  "s07.p1": "Masks, panels and helmets follow the same arithmetic as section 04, in which the dose is the irradiance times the time. An LED mask used in a controlled trial at 630 and 850 nm delivered at most 10 mW/cm², and with it 10 minutes give 6 J/cm². Full-body panels measured at 15 cm delivered 52 to 90 mW/cm², and at 30 cm, 23 to 55 mW/cm². A TikTok reviewer measured an average of about 2 mW/cm² from one mask, and with it 10 minutes give 1.2 J/cm². Since the dose that helped in the studies ranges from a few joules with the hospital laser to tens of joules in the LED skin studies, the same 10 minutes a day can deliver anything from 1.2 to 54 J/cm², depending on the device.",
  "s07.p2": "In Demidova-Rice’s mouse wounds (2007), an ordinary 635 nm lamp and a 633 nm laser made no difference, and reviews in the field conclude that an LED works when it delivers the right wavelength and dose (Kim and Calderhead, 2011; Heiskanen and Hamblin, 2018). What the in-office laser has in addition is the ability to concentrate a lot of light on a small spot, while a panel spreads weaker light over a large area.",
  "s07.p3": "To find out how many minutes your device would need, look on the box or the website for the irradiance and the distance at which it was measured. Type the number below, or choose one of the reference devices, and compare the minutes with the doses in the studies:",
  "l08.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How many minutes your device needs to deliver the studies’ dose</b><span class=\"wdo\">Type the irradiance from your device’s box, or choose one of the references, and the minutes the manufacturer tells you to use. Compare the time for each dose with the time on the box.</span>",
  "l08.cap": "Trial mask: Korean study at 630 and 850 nm, maximum 10 mW/cm² (PMC11835066), read in the abstract. 2 mW/cm² mask: measurement cited by Lab Muffin (2025). Panels: measurements by Light Therapy Insiders and LightLab International, sites with declared affiliation to the brands. Sun: integration of the ASTM G173 spectrum done for this essay. Doses: Antunes et al. (2013); WALT (2010); Zein et al. (2018); Grimes (<em>PLOS One</em>, 2025); Huang et al. (2009). Gap between advertised and measured in five panels: LightTherapyIQ.",
  "s07.p4": "The calculation shows a large difference in scale between devices. At 10 mW/cm², the controlled trial’s mask takes about 7 minutes to deliver the 4 J/cm² that the INCA laser put on each point in the mouth, and more than an hour for the median dose of the LED skin studies. A panel at 15 cm delivers 31 to 54 J/cm² in 10 minutes, and goes past the dose that worsened the mice’s wounds before the time many manufacturers recommend is up.",
  "s07.p5": "The irradiance on the box is also usually higher than the measured one. In five popular panels measured at 15 cm with a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"espectrometro\">spectrometer</span>, the irradiance came out 33% to 57% below what was advertised. The sites that took the measurements are affiliated with the brands, which donated the panels and paid for the tests. Part of the gap comes from the manufacturer’s method, which sometimes measures with the sensor pressed against the LED or with a meter made for sunlight, which overestimates LED light. The few peer-reviewed tests point the same way. In a bench study of five LED devices for home use, the power fluctuated during use, and the authors concluded that the manufacturers’ dose instructions do not allow a precise dose to be delivered (Cronshaw and colleagues, 2025). A 2026 analysis of four cosmetic masks found wide variation in the spectrum and noted that most do not publish their irradiance.",
  "s07.p6": "Distance also changes the dose, but not by the rule taught at school. A panel was measured at 15 cm, and you move back to 30 cm. Choose how much of the light you think is left:",
  "l09.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How much of a panel’s light is left when you step back</b><span class=\"wdo\">Choose how much is left at 30 cm and see the answer on the three curves. Then compare, below, each box’s number with the measured one.</span>",
  "l09.cap": "Curves: this essay’s calculation for a uniform 30 × 90 cm panel, normalized at 15 cm. Measurements at 15 and 30 cm: Light Therapy Insiders, LightTherapyIQ and LightLab International (2026), with declared affiliation to the brands. The two sets of measurements used different methods.",
  "s07.p7": "The <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"inverso\">inverse square law</span>, in which doubling the distance divides the light by four, holds for a small source. A 90 cm panel seen up close behaves like a wall of light, and only at distances greater than the panel itself does the fall approach the rule. The measurements at 15 and 30 cm fell to about 50% to 60%, close to the large panel model. Even so, standing at 60 cm instead of 15 cm divides the dose by about four, and the instructions rarely say how far away to stand.",
  "s08.step": "08 · The sun",
  "s08.h": "Ten minutes of midday sun deliver more red and infrared light than the dose of many studies, with ultraviolet included",
  "s08.p1": "The sun emits across the whole spectrum, and a large part of that light falls in the same band as the devices. At midday, under a clear sky, at a mid latitude, the reference spectrum used in solar engineering adds up to about 100 mW/cm², and about 40 mW/cm² of it lies between 600 and 1,000 nm, the red and near infrared, according to the calculation done for this essay on the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"astm\">ASTM G173</span> table. That is more than the controlled trial’s mask and between half and three quarters of a panel at 15 cm. The chart below splits each source’s light by band:",
  "l10.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How much red, infrared and ultraviolet light the sun, a panel, a mask and a lamp deliver</b><span class=\"wdo\">Compare the red and plum slice of each source, and then the time each one takes to reach 10 J/cm², on the right.</span>",
  "l10.cap": "ASTM G173-03 reference spectrum (clear sky, mid latitude, surface tilted at 37°), integrated for this essay; at midday in the Brazilian summer the total can exceed 100 mW/cm², and with clouds it drops a lot. Panel: measurements by reviewers with affiliations. Lamp and sauna: Planck’s law, this essay’s calculation. Mask: Korean trial (PMC11835066).",
  "s08.p2": "<span class=\"mark\">Ten minutes of strong sun deliver about 24 J/cm² of red and near infrared light, more than the dose of many device studies.</span>",
  "s08.p3": "The comparison changes when only the narrow band of an LED is counted, and in that band the sun is weak, because between 650 and 670 nm it delivers 2.8 mW/cm², while a panel delivers tens. Grimes (2025) converted the doses of the 27 LED skin studies into time in the sun, counting only each LED’s band, and found 36 seconds to 19 hours, with a median of 3.3 hours. Both ways of counting are legitimate, and what no study has measured is whether the broad light of the sun produces the same effects as the narrow light of an LED. The sun also brings <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"uv\">ultraviolet</span>, about 4.6 mW/cm² at midday, and it is ultraviolet that burns the skin and causes skin cancer. That is why an hour in the midday sun burns the skin, and an hour under a red LED with no ultraviolet does not. Anyone who wants the sun’s red light gets the ultraviolet with it, and sunscreen is still needed.",
  "s08.p4": "The sauna stays out of the comparison. The Finnish sauna and the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"fir\">far infrared</span> sauna heat by hot air and by radiation above 3,000 nm, and almost none of their energy falls between 600 and 1,000 nm. “Near infrared” saunas with incandescent lamps put only 15% to 18% of their light in that band, and most of it becomes heat.",
  "s09.step": "09 · Safety",
  "s09.h": "On the skin, red light causes few problems, and the serious risks are in the eyes, above all in myopia devices for children",
  "s09.p1": "At the doses used in studies, red light warms the skin only a little. With 635 and 808 nm lasers delivering 6 to 12 joules, the skin warmed by 0.7 to 1.6 °C, and the volunteers did not notice (Souza-Barros and colleagues, 2018). The 2025 expert consensus concluded that photobiomodulation is safe in adults and that red light does not damage DNA, unlike ultraviolet (Maghfour and colleagues). The skin problems that show up in the records come mostly from combining it with other treatments. In MAUDE, the system in which the FDA receives reports of problems with devices, there are 109 reports for wrinkle masks. In the 100 reports read for this essay, the word “eye” appears in 38 and “burn” in 27, and 66 concern a single model, the Shark CryoGlow, which combines LEDs and cooling. One report describes a rash, a burn and blisters after using the mask on skin treated with <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"tretinoina\">tretinoin</span>, an acid for acne and wrinkles. These reports are voluntary and do not prove cause, and they serve to show the kind of problem.",
  "s09.p2": "Skin color changes the calculation. Melanin absorbs part of the light before it reaches the dermis, and in a simulation done for this essay dark skin would lose about half of the 630 and 660 nm light in the top layer alone, while infrared would lose less. At doses above the WALT tables, an 810 nm, 200 mW laser produced 3 to 6 times more heat in dark skin than in light skin and warmed the skin by as much as 22.3 °C. Of the 13 participants with dark skin, 8 asked to stop because of discomfort (Joensen and colleagues, 2011). At WALT doses, warming stayed below 1.5 °C in every skin tone. Zakia Rahman, a Stanford dermatologist, reminded NPR that visible light can stimulate pigment, more so with the short colors, and that overuse can worsen patches such as <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"melasma\">melasma</span>, which are more common in Black and brown skin. The WALT dose tables were calibrated on white skin, and in the trial that led to Valeda’s authorization no non-white patient received the treatment.",
  "s09.p3": "The eyes are the weak point, because the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"retina\">retina</span> receives light focused by the eye’s lens. Many masks and panels come with protective goggles in the box, and in 2019 Neutrogena recalled a blue light acne mask over a theoretical risk for people with certain eye diseases or taking drugs that increase sensitivity to light. The most serious case is childhood myopia, in which the light is deliberately aimed at children’s retinas. In 2026, Ostrin and Schill, of the University of Houston, measured four myopia devices, all with 3-minute sessions, and calculated the time until each one exceeded the safe <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"limite\">exposure limit</span> of the American standards:",
  "l11.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">In how many seconds each myopia device exceeds the safe limit for the retina, in a 3-minute session</b><span class=\"wdo\">Compare each bar with the line for the 180-second session. The bars that end before it exceed the limit during the session.</span>",
  "l11.cap": "Ostrin and Schill (<em>JAMA Ophthalmology</em>, 2026), bench test funded by the National Eye Institute; the Future Vision time comes from Healio’s coverage (06/30/2026). Ostrin et al. (2024), who measured 7.2 W/cm² on the retina with the Sky-n1201a. Liu et al. (<em>JAMA Ophthalmol</em>, 2023). Chinese regulation as described by Wang, Wang and Wong (<em>JAMA Ophthalmol</em>, 2025). The ratios between the session and the time to the limit are this essay’s calculations.",
  "s09.p4": "<span class=\"mark\">Two devices sold to slow myopia in children exceeded the safe limit for the retina in less than 3 seconds, and each session lasts 3 minutes.</span>",
  "s09.p5": "Eyerising, whose medical director is Mingguang He, senior author of several of the myopia trials, replied that more than 110,000 children have been treated with no confirmed permanent retinal injury when the protocol was followed, and that the standards were written for accidental exposure. Ostrin called the reply fair and incomplete, because a subtle retinal injury may not show up in a routine exam. The large 2022 trial reported no serious events, and the case published in 2023, of a 12-year-old girl who lost vision in both eyes after five months of use, with damage to the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"fotorreceptores\">photoreceptors</span>, is an isolated report. The effect on eye growth shows up in the trials, and the safety of these devices has not yet been settled.",
  "s09.p6": "Other precautions have less data. Drugs that increase sensitivity to light, such as tetracyclines, isotretinoin and tretinoin, appear on manufacturers’ lists of cautions. On tumors, the common advice is not to apply light over a known tumor without talking to the oncologist, and the most reassuring data point is the INCA trial. Pregnancy and light over the thyroid have not been studied, and no medical society states that there is a risk or that there is none.",
  "s10.step": "10 · Before you buy",
  "s10.h": "A red light device makes sense only for the uses on the top rungs, with the light and the minutes the studies used",
  "s10.p1": "Most masks sold as FDA approved were only cleared by it. <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"k510\">510(k)</span> clearance requires the manufacturer to show that the device is similar to another one already sold legally, and it can be granted on bench tests, without a clinical efficacy trial. The FDA itself says that clearance does not mean approval and that saying “FDA approved” in this case misleads the consumer. De Novo authorization creates a new category for low to moderate risk devices and requires a clinical study, like Valeda’s. <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"pma\">PMA</span> approval, the most demanding, requires proof of safety and efficacy, and the search done for this essay found no photobiomodulation device approved this way. In Brazil, Anvisa classifies light devices with a therapeutic or aesthetic purpose as health products, in risk classes I to IV, and has not issued a specific alert about unregistered LED masks:",
  "l12.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">What each FDA and Anvisa seal guarantees about a red light device</b><span class=\"wdo\">Read each card from top to bottom, from what the manufacturer had to show to the sentence the ad should use.</span>",
  "l12.cap": "FDA, openFDA classification and 510(k) databases, queried on 11/14/2026; FDA DEN230083. Pure Global, on “cleared”, “granted” and “approved”. Anvisa, RDC 751/2022 and Technical Note GQUIP 002/2017. ASA, 11/05/2025; BBB National Programs, NAD, 12/11/2019; FTC, Willow Curve, 2020.",
  "s10.p2": "David Ozog, a dermatologist at Henry Ford Health, told NPR that clearance shows that many devices are safe, without showing that they work, and that he has tested devices that did not emit enough light to have any effect. That is why the box matters more than the seal. The label below gathers what this essay has shown into a sheet for the device you own or want to buy. If you typed the numbers from your box in section 07, they are already filled in:",
  "l13.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Your device’s label, filled in with what the studies say</b><span class=\"wdo\">Pick the use you want, fill in the fields from the box and mark your situation. The label returns the evidence rung for that use, the minutes for the studies’ dose and the warnings that apply to you.</span>",
  "l13.cap": "Rungs: the ladder in section 05. Doses: sections 04 and 07. Warnings: Ostrin and Schill (2026); MAUDE/FDA; Rahman, to NPR (04/13/2026); Neutrogena recall (2019). The label summarizes the studies cited in the essay and does not replace a medical consultation.",
  "s10.p3": "The mask that made searches hit a record in April 2026 works with the right physics, because it emits red and near infrared light inside the band that skin lets through. Its light acts in the first few millimeters of skin, at doses that almost no box states in a way anyone can check, for uses that sit on the bottom rungs of the ladder. The same physics, in a 660 nm laser with a hospital protocol, cut severe mouth sores from about 40 to about 6 in every 100 head and neck patients in the INCA trial. In Brazil, that treatment was added to the SUS procedure table in 2025.",
  "m.step": "Note on method",
  "m.l1": "The mask fad opens the essay in section 00 and returns only at the end. The rest of the text deals with red light in general, and no brand, influencer or device is followed point by point.",
  "m.l2": "The essay moves from physics to the cell, from the cell to the dose, from the dose to the evidence in people, and only then to the devices, the sun, safety and buying. That way the reader reaches the evidence ladder already able to read nanometers, irradiance and dose.",
  "m.l3": "The penetration numbers come from living volunteers (cheek and hand), from cadavers and dry skulls, and from simulations. Cadavers have no circulating blood, which absorbs more light, so the percentages measured in them should be read as a ceiling. The depths by wavelength, in the chart of the cheek, the hand and the skull, come from a simulation done for this essay with average skin parameters, and they serve to show the shape of the curve.",
  "m.l4": "The dose points in section 04 come from studies in cells and animals, gathered in two reviews by Huang and colleagues (2009 and 2011). The dashed curves join the points and are not statistical fits. In people, almost no study has tested several doses of the same device.",
  "m.l5": "The evidence ladder has five rungs: a guideline recommends it, moderate effect, weak signal, studies with no effect, and no study in people. Each use was classified by the best evidence found in trials in people, and the cards say what that evidence is. The uses with a guideline and the advertised uses involve different devices, and the cards say when the study was done with a hospital device or a home one.",
  "m.l6": "The panel irradiances come from product review sites affiliated with the brands, because there is no independent peer-reviewed test of full-body panels. The peer-reviewed tests cited are of handheld devices, masks and myopia devices.",
  "m.l7": "Some calculations were done for this essay from published data. They are the light times in the wound study (dose divided by 100 mW/cm²); the minutes for each device in the calculator in section 07 (dose divided by irradiance times 0.06); the 10-minute doses of a panel, a mask and the sun; the sum of the sun between 600 and 1,000 nm on the reference spectrum; the distance curves of a uniform panel; the fraction of an incandescent lamp’s light in the band, by Planck’s law; the conversion of 19.9 mm to about 2 points out of 10; the 1.8 cm per site in the fat study; and the ratios between the myopia session and the time to the limit.",
  "m.l8": "Google Trends: the index is relative within each query, collected on 11/14/2026 with pytrends, with each group of terms in a new session so that the results of one country did not mix with those of another. The Brazilian series for “red light therapy” is small and noisy, so the essay uses “laserterapia” and “LED facial” for Brazil. FDA: the public openFDA database, product codes OHS, OLP, OAP, ILY and NHN. MAUDE reports are voluntary and do not prove cause.",
  "m.l9": "Some numbers come from a paper’s abstract, from news reports or from product review sites, without a reading of the full text. Among them are Kardashian’s presents, CurrentBody’s revenue, the <em>Cureus</em> survey of posts, the panel measurements, the Future Vision time, Eyerising’s reply and the doctors’ remarks to NPR. When one of these numbers appears in a chart, it is marked as approximate.",
  "m.l10": "This essay is not medical advice. Light devices near the eyes, above all in children, should be used under the supervision of an ophthalmologist.",
  "f.step": "Main sources",
  "f.refs": "\n  <b>Starting point.</b> Google Trends, queries of 11/14/2026. openFDA, 510(k) and MAUDE, queried on 11/14/2026. Marie Claire, on Kim Kardashian and the masks. The Beauty Tech Group, 2025 results. Merkle et al., <em>Cureus</em> 2026. Shen and Zhao, <em>JMIR Dermatology</em> 2025.<br>\n  <b>Physics and penetration.</b> Prahl, Oregon Medical Laser Center (hemoglobin). Hale and Querry, <em>Applied Optics</em> 1973 (water). Jacques, <em>Phys Med Biol</em> 2013. Jagdeo et al., <em>PLoS One</em> 2012. Tedford et al., <em>Lasers Surg Med</em> 2015. Ash et al., <em>Lasers Med Sci</em> 2017. Lapchak et al., 2015. Salehpour et al., 2019. ASTM G173-03 (solar spectrum). Souza-Barros et al., 2018. Joensen et al., 2011.<br>\n  <b>History and mechanism.</b> Mester, Szende and Gärtner, <em>Radiobiol Radiother</em> 1968. Whelan et al., <em>J Clin Laser Med Surg</em> 2001. Anders, Lanzafame and Arany, <em>Photomed Laser Surg</em> 2015. MeSH D028022. Karu, 1999. Karu and Kolyakov, <em>Photomed Laser Surg</em> 2005. Mason, Nicholls and Cooper, <em>BBA</em> 2014. Passarella et al., <em>FEBS Lett</em> 1984. de Freitas and Hamblin, 2016. Chen et al., <em>PLoS One</em> 2011. Wang et al., 2017 and 2018. Lima et al., 2019. Quirk and Whelan, 2020.<br>\n  <b>Dose.</b> Huang, Chen, Carroll and Hamblin, <em>Dose Response</em> 2009. Huang et al., <em>Dose Response</em> 2011. Demidova-Rice et al., <em>Photomed Laser Surg</em> 2007. Lanzafame et al., 2007. Sharma et al., 2011. Zein, Selting and Hamblin, 2018. WALT, 2010 dose table. Grimes, <em>PLOS One</em> 2025.<br>\n  <b>Clinical evidence.</b> Zadik et al., <em>Support Care Cancer</em> 2019 (MASCC/ISOO). Oberoi et al., <em>PLoS One</em> 2014. Antunes et al., <em>Oral Oncology</em> 2013 and 2017. Hu et al., <em>Support Care Cancer</em> 2026. Rodrigues et al., 2024. Lueangarun et al., 2021. Adil and Godwin, <em>JAAD</em> 2017. Chow et al., <em>Lancet</em> 2009. Stausholm et al., <em>BMJ Open</em> 2019. Naterstad et al., <em>BMJ Open</em> 2022. NICE NG226. Dutra et al., 2022. Boyer et al., <em>Retina</em> 2024. Jaffe et al., 2026. FDA DEN230083. Jiang et al., <em>Ophthalmology</em> 2022. Xie et al., 2026. Barbaric et al., Cochrane 2016. Wunsch and Matuschka, <em>Photomed Laser Surg</em> 2014. Li et al., 2018. Rasul et al., 2026. Jackson et al., 2009. Elm et al., 2011. Chun et al., 2025. Seok et al., 2026. Zhao et al., 2012. Höfling et al., <em>Lasers Med Sci</em> 2013. Son et al., <em>Syst Rev</em> 2025. Maghfour et al., <em>JAAD</em> 2025.<br>\n  <b>Devices.</b> Cronshaw et al., <em>Dentistry Journal</em> 2025. Baeza-Moyano, Bernardez and Sola, <em>Photochem Photobiol Sci</em> 2026. Korean LED mask trial (PMC11835066). Lab Muffin, “The science of red light masks” (2025). LightTherapyIQ and Light Therapy Insiders, with declared affiliation. NPR, “Is red light therapy healthy or all hype”, 04/13/2026.<br>\n  <b>Safety and regulation.</b> Ostrin and Schill, <em>JAMA Ophthalmology</em> 2026. Ostrin et al., 2024. Liu et al., <em>JAMA Ophthalmol</em> 2023. Healio, 06/30/2026. Wang, Wang and Wong, <em>JAMA Ophthalmol</em> 2025. American Academy of Ophthalmology. FDA, MAUDE. Neutrogena recall, AOP 2019. ASA, 11/05/2025. NAD/BBB, 2019. FTC, Willow Curve 2020. Anvisa, RDC 751/2022. Ministry of Health ordinance SAES/MS 3.035/2025.\n  ",
  "f.foot": "Does red light therapy work? · interactive essay · November 2026"
};
